'Autoresearch', but for theoretical science? I formalized my blog posts on steepest descent convergence bounds and hyperparameter scaling laws in Lean using Codex. This started as an art project, but I ended up having similar (almost exactly the same) results as prior work while having much weaker assumptions, so I think I may be onto something here.

This is also a glimpse of how theoretical science might look in the near future. LLMs are now good at 'reverse mathematics', where instead of asking what results we can get from a set of assumptions, we ask, 'what's the minimal set of assumptions/axioms/postulates we need to reproduce (empirical) results?'

@leloykun: "Excited results!! I was also working on this direction a few months back but only managed to reach the necessary step before this. I got similar results to @dakovalev1's, but without the need to go back-and-forth between dual norms and the Frobenius norm which makes the coefficients loose. On top of that, I also added handling of Nesterov momentum and decoupled weight decay. I think the next natural step is to use last-iterate bounds instead of expected bounds so we can also handle variable (decaying) learning rates."

Got back to nanoGPTJAX after a while. More improvements include implementing the famous RoPE-NoPE (local-global attention) pattern, which I surprisingly prefer even for small models. The KVCache has been simplified and is now fully intuitive, along with bug fixes and performance improvements.

Though I want to work on the Quantization API, I continue to conduct fun data-based experiments, which is utterly satisfying. 

@A_K_Nain: "More improvements: 1. Cautious weight decay with Muon - pure gold! 2. GPU flags for better performance 3. Improved prefetching with autotune 4. Reusable buffers to save on copies 5. Custom gradient accumulation (optax MultiSteps had a bug)!"

Though we used the tokenized Fineweb (from modded-nanogpt) up to this point, I would rather switch to a better dataset. ClimbMix is extremely good, but I would prefer to use it once all the TODO tasks are completed. Quick experimentation requires a dataset similar to Fineweb 10B, but with better quality.

I also want to simplify the dataloader and plan to tokenize data in a much better way; Grain is an extremely powerful library! 

@A_K_Nain: "Got back to nanoGPTJAX after a while. More improvements: 1. Implemented the famous RoPE-NoPE (local-global attention) pattern. Ran a few experiments and surprisingly I am biased towards it even for small models. 2. Simplified the KVCache. It is fully intuitive now. 3. Bug fixes and performance improvements. Though at this point I want to work on Quantization API, I keep doing fun data-based experiments. Utterly satisfying!"

Been saying this for a while. Use the coding agent at the right point in time, and it unlocks huge value for a good developer.

@kennethreitz42: "Everyone assumes the best AI coding workflow is: let AI generate V1, then maintain it by hand. I'm finding the opposite works better. Write the first version yourself — pour your taste and instincts into it. Then let AI handle the features, cleanup, and maintenance. A hand-written codebase is a style guide written in code. AI reads it and extends it faithfully. Without that foundation, everything regresses toward the mean. The human provides the soul. The AI provides the scale."

The linked article discusses how starting with a human-written codebase allows AI tools to better understand and extend the code, leading to more effective collaboration between human developers and AI.

